\chapter{Booting the Machine}
\label{ch:boot}

This chapter follows the processor from the first instruction to the kernel's \texttt{kmain}. On the PC, GRUB loads the kernel
and starts it in 32-bit protected mode; here the emulator or the Raspberry Pi firmware starts it directly, with the MMU off, at
exception level EL1 or EL2. The boot code builds the first page tables, drops to EL1, jumps to the high addresses at which the
kernel is linked, and sets up the exception vectors. Platform differences between qemu's \texttt{virt} machine and the
Raspberry Pi 4B are kept small (\texttt{plat.h}); everything else comes from the device tree at run time.

@
\section{Boot code for qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt and for the Raspberry Pi 4B}
\label{src:start.S}

qemu enters at the *physical* address [[0x40080000]] with the MMU off, at EL1 (or EL2); the Pi firmware loads
[[kernel8.img]] at [[0x80000]] and starts it at EL2 (see [[ulix.ld]] and "[[make PLATFORM]]=rpi4" in the Makefile).
The kernel is linked at [[0xFFFF000000000000]] + PA, so everything up to the jump to
the high address must be position independent (adr, no absolute symbol loads).

@

